a launch triggered through a PendingIntent received from a different app. A Background Activity Launch occurs when an app not in the foreground, with no visible activities, or a PendingIntent received from a different app attempts to start a new activity.\n\n**Opt-in rules:** For a launch to succeed, either the app that created the PendingIntent or the app that sends it must opt in. For apps targeting Android 15 or higher, the creator no longer grants this privilege implicitly. Apps targeting Android 15 or higher will default to no longer implicitly granting background activity launch privileges to PendingIntents they create. The sender can opt in through ActivityOptions, using the setPendingIntentBackgroundActivityStartMode method on the options passed to send().\n\n**Implication for widgets:** Widget clicks are typically handled by the launcher, and the template's PendingIntent is sent from the launcher's process. Whether a broadcast-receiver-then-startActivity \"trampoline\" still gets BAL privileges depends on the creator and sender opt-in settings, and this search didn't cover that case. The official Android BAL and PendingIntent docs are the best next place to check, especially the creator-side opt-in section."],"durationSeconds":6.010104330000002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MdXtGZBndGgGo3m1TPmaHp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
